## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP with non-overlapping selections and per-subarray costs, mirroring real scenarios like batch processing, budget allocation, or ad-slot optimization where exactly k intervals are chosen from a sequence.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping k-interval DP
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Maintaining a running best to optimize transitions from O(n) to O(1)
- Reconstruction of exact intervals

## DP Recurrence
Let pref[i] = sum of first i elements (pref[0] = 0).

**Transition:**
dp[i][t] = max(
  dp[i-1][t],                                    // Don't end a subarray at i
  best[t] + pref[i]                              // End a subarray at i
)

where best[t] = max_{1 ≤ l ≤ i} (dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1])

**Initialization:**
dp[0][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

**Answer:** dp[n][k]

## Reconstruction
Track which choice was made at each dp[i][t]. If dp[i][t] came from dp[i-1][t], no subarray ends at i. Otherwise, find l where dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1] = best[t], and record subarray (l..i).

## Complexity
- Time: O(nk) with O(1) transitions using running best
- Space: O(nk) for dp table, O(nk) for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = [0];
  for (let x of A) pref.push(pref[pref.length - 1] + x);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip position i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 0; // 0 = skip
      }

      // Start/extend subarray ending at i
      if (t > 0) {
        let best = -Infinity;
        for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]) - c;
          if (val > best) best = val;
        }
        if (best > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = best;
          choice[i][t] = 1; // 1 = take subarray ending at i
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const subarrays = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (choice[i][t] === 1) {
      let l = 1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[j - 1]) - c;
        if (val === dp[i][t]) {
          l = j;
          break;
        }
      }
      subarrays.unshift([l - 1, i - 1]); // Convert to 0-indexed
      t--;
    }
    i--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Optimized O(nk) Version
The inner loop can be optimized by maintaining a running best:
```javascript
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i - 1][t - 1] - pref[i - 1]);
    dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], best[t] + pref[i] - c);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to structure a dynamic programming solution for exact-k resource selection with non-overlapping intervals. It requires combining weighted interval scheduling with a prefix DP over both task count and index, along with binary search for efficient predecessor lookup and clean reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with exact k constraint
- Prefix DP over tasks (i) and count (t)
- Precomputing p[i] via binary search for O(log n) lookups
- Backtracking through DP table for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function weightedIntervalScheduling(tasks, K) {
  // Sort by end time
  tasks.sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = tasks.length;

  // Precompute p[i] - last non-overlapping task
  const ends = tasks.map(t => t.end);
  const p = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1, res = -1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor) / 2((lo + hi);
      if (tasks[mid].end <= tasks[i].start) {
        res = mid;
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
      }
    }
    p[i] = res;
  }

  // DP table: dp[i][t] = max value using first i tasks for t selections
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(K + 1).fill(0));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= K; t++) {
      // Skip task i-1
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      // Take task i-1
      if (p[i - 1] !== -1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[p[i - 1] + 1][t - 1] + tasks[i - 1].value);
      } else if (t === 1) {
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], tasks[i - 1].value);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const chosen = [];
  let i = n, t = K;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t]) {
      i--;
    } else {
      chosen.push(i - 1);
      const prev = p[i - 1];
      i = prev + 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][K], chosenTasks: chosen.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP with partitioning and additive segment costs; uses prefix sums to optimize the transition to O(nk).

## Key Concepts

- DP on partitioning into k segments
- Prefix sums to compute segment costs in O(1)
- State transition optimization with a running max and backpointers
- Reconstruction of cut points

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartitionScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  if (k <= 0 || k > n) return {score: 0, cuts: []};
  const diff = new Array(n-1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n-1; i++) diff[i] = Math.abs(A[i+1] - A[i]);
  const pref = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = pref[i-1];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity, bestP = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const p = i - 1;
      const val = dp[p][t-1] - pref[p];
      if (val > best) { best = val; bestP = p; }
      dp[i][t] = pref[i-1] + best;
      take[i][t] = bestP;
    }
  }
  let cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = take[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = p;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {score: dp[n][k], cuts};
}
```

